# Component Architecture

Organize UI using layered components.

## ui/

Lowest level components.

Contains only primitive UI.

Examples:

- Button
- Input
- Badge
- Avatar
- Dialog
- Sheet
- Card
- Tabs

Rules:

- No business logic.
- No API calls.
- No feature-specific code.
- Reusable across the entire project.

---

## shared/

Reusable composed components built from ui/.

Examples:

- Navbar
- Sidebar
- CameraPreview
- Countdown
- SessionCard
- GalleryGrid
- UploadProgress

Rules:

- May combine multiple ui components.
- May contain presentation logic.
- Must remain feature-agnostic.

---

## module/

Feature-specific components and business logic.

Examples:

- auth/
- session/
- capture/
- gallery/
- experience/

Contains:

- feature components
- hooks
- local stores
- API integration
- feature utilities

Rules:

- Owns business logic.
- Can use shared and ui.
- Should not depend on other modules unless explicitly shared.

---

## page/

Contains page composition only.

Examples:

- HomePage
- SessionPage
- GalleryPage
- DashboardPage

Rules:

- Compose layouts and modules.
- No business logic.
- Keep Next.js app/page.tsx files minimal.

Example:

app/session/[id]/page.tsx

export default function Page() {
return <SessionPage />;
}

---

## layout/

Application layouts.

Examples:

- DashboardLayout
- PhotoboothLayout
- AuthLayout

Rules:

- Defines page structure.
- Handles navigation, sidebar, headers and slots.
- No feature business logic.

---

## hoc/

Application wrappers.

Examples:

- AuthProvider
- QueryProvider
- ThemeProvider
- SessionProvider

Rules:

- Contains providers, guards and application wrappers.
- Loaded globally.
- Avoid placing feature logic here.

---

# Dependency Rule

Allowed:

ui
↑
shared
↑
module
↑
page
↑
app router

layout can use:

- ui
- shared

hoc can use:

- providers
- shared utilities

Never import downward.

ui -> module ❌
shared -> module ❌
page -> app ❌

Always keep dependencies flowing upward.
